window.ASN_QA = [{"id":1,"category":"Overview","question":"What was the main research question?","answer":"We asked whether baseline proteinuria changes the association between serum uric acid and subsequent decline in estimated GFR."},{"id":2,"category":"Overview","question":"Why did you focus on proteinuria?","answer":"Proteinuria may identify people whose kidneys are more vulnerable. We tested whether the uric acid association differs according to proteinuria status."},{"id":3,"category":"Overview","question":"What is new about this study?","answer":"We examined this association in a community cohort using repeated kidney function measurements, nonlinear modeling of uric acid, and a formal interaction test."},{"id":4,"category":"Cohort","question":"Who was included in the analysis?","answer":"We analyzed 8,266 adults aged 20 years or older from coastal communities in Iwate, Japan."},{"id":5,"category":"Cohort","question":"When were the participants followed?","answer":"The baseline survey began in September 2011, and follow-up surveys were conducted annually through 2020. Mean follow-up was 7.1 years."},{"id":6,"category":"Cohort","question":"Who was excluded?","answer":"We excluded people with prior myocardial infarction or stroke, gout or urate-lowering therapy, no follow-up estimated GFR data, or missing baseline covariates."},{"id":7,"category":"Cohort","question":"Could excluding people without follow-up data have affected the findings?","answer":"Yes. We excluded 1,334 people without follow-up estimated GFR data, so selection bias is possible."},{"id":8,"category":"Measurements","question":"What were the exposure and outcome?","answer":"The exposure was baseline serum uric acid. The outcome was the annual change in estimated GFR, expressed as an estimated GFR slope."},{"id":9,"category":"Measurements","question":"How did you define proteinuria?","answer":"In the main analysis, proteinuria meant trace or greater on a baseline urine dipstick test."},{"id":10,"category":"Measurements","question":"How many participants had proteinuria?","answer":"At baseline, 371 of the 8,266 participants had proteinuria, or 4.5 percent."},{"id":11,"category":"Measurements","question":"Was proteinuria measured more than once?","answer":"No. It was assessed using a single daytime spot urine sample, so we could not distinguish transient from persistent proteinuria."},{"id":12,"category":"Measurements","question":"Did you measure the urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio?","answer":"No. We used dipstick proteinuria and did not have a quantitative albumin-to-creatinine ratio for this analysis."},{"id":13,"category":"Methods","question":"Why did you use a linear mixed-effects model?","answer":"We had repeated estimated GFR measurements. The model included a random intercept and a random time slope for each participant."},{"id":14,"category":"Methods","question":"How did you model serum uric acid?","answer":"We modeled it as a continuous variable using restricted cubic splines, with knots at the tenth, fiftieth, and ninetieth percentiles."},{"id":15,"category":"Methods","question":"Why did you use splines instead of a straight line?","answer":"Splines allowed the estimated association to be nonlinear without forcing it to follow a straight line."},{"id":16,"category":"Methods","question":"How did you test whether proteinuria modified the association?","answer":"We tested the interaction among time, spline-transformed serum uric acid, and baseline proteinuria in the mixed-effects model."},{"id":17,"category":"Methods","question":"Which factors did you adjust for?","answer":"We adjusted for baseline estimated GFR, age, sex, body mass index, blood pressure, diabetes, smoking, alcohol use, dyslipidemia, and disaster-related home damage or flooding."},{"id":18,"category":"Results","question":"Were there baseline differences between the proteinuria groups?","answer":"Yes. Participants with proteinuria were older on average and had higher blood pressure and uric acid and lower baseline estimated GFR."},{"id":19,"category":"Results","question":"What did you find in participants without proteinuria?","answer":"Their estimated GFR slope changed little across uric acid levels. The mean slope was minus 1.11 milliliters per minute per 1.73 square meters per year."},{"id":20,"category":"Results","question":"What did you find in participants with proteinuria?","answer":"The estimated GFR slope was least negative at 5.0 milligrams per deciliter, at minus 1.27, and reached minus 2.04 at 7.8 milligrams per deciliter. Both slopes are per year."},{"id":21,"category":"Results","question":"Was the interaction statistically significant?","answer":"Yes. The interaction between serum uric acid and proteinuria for estimated GFR slope had a P value below 0.001."},{"id":22,"category":"Results","question":"Did the association appear to be linear in the proteinuria group?","answer":"No. The association was nonlinear, with steeper estimated GFR decline at higher serum uric acid levels."},{"id":23,"category":"Results","question":"What do the shaded areas in the main figure show?","answer":"They show 95 percent confidence intervals around the estimated GFR slopes from the model."},{"id":24,"category":"Results","question":"Which subgroup finding remained significant after correction?","answer":"Only baseline estimated GFR showed significant heterogeneity after Bonferroni correction. With baseline estimated GFR below 60, the proteinuria group had steeper decline across the displayed uric acid range."},{"id":25,"category":"Results","question":"Did age, sex, or other subgroups show the same heterogeneity?","answer":"No other prespecified factor, including age, sex, body mass index, blood pressure, or diabetes, showed significant heterogeneity after Bonferroni correction."},{"id":26,"category":"Results","question":"Did the result persist with a stricter definition of proteinuria?","answer":"Yes. Defining proteinuria as dipstick 1 plus or greater gave a similar overall pattern. That definition identified 146 participants."},{"id":27,"category":"Interpretation","question":"Why did you limit the stricter-definition analysis to the overall cohort?","answer":"Only 146 participants met the stricter proteinuria definition, so further subgroup analyses would have been too sparse."},{"id":28,"category":"Interpretation","question":"Does the low point near 5.0 mean that we should target that uric acid level?","answer":"No. This is an observational curve, not a treatment trial, and it does not establish an optimal uric acid target."},{"id":29,"category":"Interpretation","question":"Could reduced kidney function itself raise uric acid?","answer":"Yes. Reverse causation is possible. We adjusted for baseline estimated GFR, but that cannot fully resolve the issue."},{"id":30,"category":"Interpretation","question":"What is your main clinical takeaway?","answer":"Higher uric acid was associated with faster kidney function decline mainly when proteinuria was present. This does not show that urate-lowering treatment will prevent decline; independent replication is needed."}];
